// Every primitive the kernel can name, and which of them the VM implements.
//
// THE NAMES ARE THE CONTRACT. packages/Core declares a primitive by pragma:
//
//     + aNumber [
//         <primitive: IntAddPrimitive>
//         ...the general case, in Smalltalk...
//     ]
//
// so this list is not the VM's private numbering, it is the vocabulary the
// image speaks. It was extracted from packages/ rather than invented: 173
// distinct names in 186 declarations.
//
// ONE entry is not from packages/ and says so at its line: PrintValuePrimitive,
// which is how the VM's BUILT-IN kernel shows a value before packages/Core
// exists to be loaded. It is deliberately not wired into packages/Core, whose
// printNl goes through Transcript and a buffered stream; a C primitive writing
// straight to the descriptor would interleave with that buffer.
//
// An X-macro, included to build the enum, the table, and the DECLARATIONS of
// the implementations, so a name and its implementation cannot drift apart. The
// old VM kept a positional array whose comment said in capitals never to reorder
// it; here the position is generated from the name.
//
// TWO MACROS, because the two states are different things and not one thing with
// a null in it:
//
//   PRIMITIVE(id, "KernelName", implementation)
//   PRIMITIVE_ABSENT(id, "KernelName")
//
// PRIMITIVE_ABSENT means DECLARED BY THE KERNEL, NOT IMPLEMENTED HERE. That is
// not an error: the method still compiles, the primitive attempt is simply not
// emitted, and the method runs the Smalltalk fallback it already carries. Which
// is also how this file doubles as the parity checklist -- the count of
// PRIMITIVE entries is the honest measure of how much of the kernel can run.
//
// It used to be one macro with NULL in the third column, and splitting it is
// what lets runtime/primitives/Shared.h GENERATE the declaration of every
// implementation from this same list: `Value NULL(Value *, uint64_t);` is not a
// prototype, so as long as absence was spelled NULL the declarations had to be a
// SECOND list of the same fact, maintained by hand beside this one. It also
// makes absence a NAMED case rather than a null pointer the reader has to
// interpret, which is the rule this VM already applies to an unknown shape.
//
// Every include site must define BOTH macros. Defining only one is a compile
// error at the first line that uses the other, which is the intended behaviour:
// a site that silently skipped the absent entries would renumber the enum.

PRIMITIVE_ABSENT(ArrayEquals, "ArrayEqualsPrimitive")  // String>>= aCollection
PRIMITIVE(At, "AtPrimitive", primAt)  // Object>>at: anInteger; Object>>basicAt: anInteger
PRIMITIVE(AtPut, "AtPutPrimitive", primAtPut)  // Object>>at: anInteger put: anObject; Object>>basicAt: anInteger put: anObject
